    <!-- Unit Overview -->
    <section class="welcome-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-compass section-icon"></i> Unit Overview</h2>
        <p>of Computer Science Unit Foundation Concepts The unit focuses on Interconnectivity and Standardisation. It explores how heterogeneous systems communicate through common rules (Protocols) and the physical and logical architectures required to move data globally. Key themes include the Abstraction of Layers, the Efficiency of Packet Switching, and the Robustness of Digital Defense.</p>
    </section>

    <!-- Syllabus Links -->
    <section class="content-section">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-bookmark section-icon"></i> What You'll Learn</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li>
                    A2.1: Network types (LAN, WAN, PAN, VPN) and digital infrastructure (Cloud, Edge, Distributed). Functions of devices (Router, Switch, WAP, Gateway) and core protocols (TCP, UDP, HTTP/S, DHCP).
                </li>
                <li>
                    A2.2: Topologies (Star, Mesh, Hybrid). Networking models (Client-Server vs. P2P) and logical segmentation (VLANs).
                </li>
                <li>
                    A2.3: Transmission media (Fibre, Copper, Wireless). IP Addressing (v4/v6, Static/Dynamic, Public/Private). NAT mechanics and the step-by-step process of Packet Switching.
                </li>
                <li>
                    A2.4: Security: Firewalls (filtering rules), Encryption (Symmetric vs. Asymmetric), and the role of Digital Certificates.
                </li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

