    <!-- Watch Out For -->
    <section class="exam-section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #fff3cd 0%, #ffe5a0 100%); border-left-color: #ffc107;">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-triangle-exclamation section-icon" style="color: #856404;"></i> Common Misconceptions</h2>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Internet vs. Web:</strong> Confusing the physical infrastructure (Internet) with the service (WWW).</li>
            <li><strong>Switches vs. Routers:</strong> Believing they perform the same task; students must distinguish between MAC-based local delivery and IP-based global routing.</li>
            <li><strong>WiFi = Internet:</strong> Assuming a wireless connection inherently provides internet access, ignoring the role of the gateway.</li>
            <li><strong>Encryption:</strong> Thinking Symmetric encryption is "weak" rather than just having a different use-case (speed) compared to Asymmetric (key exchange).</li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <!-- Big Ideas -->
    <section class="content-section" style="border-top-color: var(--accent-color);">
        <h2><i class="fa-solid fa-lightbulb section-icon"></i> Big Ideas to Consider</h2>
        <div class="learning-points">
            <ul>
                <li><strong>Standardisation as Shared Knowledge:</strong> Protocols are "agreements." How does the global reliance on a single set of rules (TCP/IP) reflect a consensus-based model of knowledge?</li>
                <li><strong>Digital Divide:</strong> Does the physical infrastructure of a network dictate who can "know" or participate in global society?</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </section>

